# APEX-OS — let the desktop power menu arm a one-shot reboot into Windows.
#
# The shell's "Boot into Windows" button runs src/scripts/PowerControl.sh as the
# logged-in user; setting an EFI variable needs root, and a password prompt is not
# something a compositor-spawned script can present. This rule is what the comment
# in PowerControl.sh has always claimed existed — APEX-OS never actually shipped
# one, which is half the reason that button has never worked (apex-logs 15).
#
# Deliberately narrow: exactly two invocations of one image-owned helper.
# The empty string after the first form means "with no arguments at all". Without
# it, sudoers grants the command with ANY arguments, which is a much wider grant
# than intended even for a helper that currently ignores them.
#
# This is not a meaningful escalation. /usr/libexec/apex-boot-windows accepts no
# caller-supplied paths and no boot-entry number: it resolves the Windows entry
# itself, verifies the loader exists, and its only privileged effects are setting
# EFI BootNext to that verified entry and rebooting. An unprivileged local user can
# already reboot the machine through logind, and BootNext is consumed after one
# boot, so nothing about the persistent boot order changes.
#
# wheel rather than every local user: this is an administrative action on firmware
# state, and the APEX installer puts the primary account in wheel.
%wheel ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/libexec/apex-boot-windows "", /usr/libexec/apex-boot-windows --check
